# Pinned Trivy vulnerability-DB snapshot (WARP-243).
#
# docker-build.yml's Trivy image gate scans against THIS exact DB digest so a
# given commit's scan verdict is reproducible — it cannot flip to red on DB
# drift alone (Debian point-release lag, newly-published npm/Go advisories for
# already-pinned deps). Bumping the pin is a deliberate PR: it re-surfaces the
# real fixable-vuln set, which must then be reconciled with .trivyignore in the
# SAME PR (any newly-fixable HIGH/CRITICAL is either patched via the dep bump
# or triaged with a burn-down note). That makes DB refresh a reviewable event
# instead of a random CI failure.
#
# Resolve the current digest for a bump:
#   TOKEN=$(curl -s "https://ghcr.io/token?scope=repository:aquasecurity/trivy-db:pull&service=ghcr.io" | jq -r .token)
#   curl -sI -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
#     -H "Accept: application/vnd.oci.image.manifest.v1+json" \
#     https://ghcr.io/v2/aquasecurity/trivy-db/manifests/2 \
#     | awk -tolower'/docker-content-digest/{print $2}'
#
# Refresh monthly (WARP-3667): scripts/check-vuln-exceptions.sh fails CI when
# the snapshot below is more than 35 days old, so the pin cannot silently go
# stale. A refresh PR changes this file AND re-baselines .trivyignore (new
# entries carry an `exp:` date, 90 days out); it is never a bare pin bump.
#
# Snapshot pinned: 2026-10-04 (schema v2; tag `2` created 2026-10-04T14:35:33Z).
